# MTK's audio HAL keeps its per-stream runtime state in properties. Stock's
# compiled policy has
#   (allow mtk_hal_audio audiohal_prop (property_service (set)))
# over vendor.streamout. / vendor.streamin. / vendor.a2dp. / vendor.audiohal. /
# persist.vendor.audiohal.  Without a device type these fall through AOSP's
# `vendor.` -> vendor_default_prop catch-all
# (system/sepolicy/private/property_contexts:168-175).
#
# WHY THE DEVICE TYPE IS STILL RIGHT -- read this before "simplifying" any of
# the five sites in this tree that used to say vendor_default_prop is
# unsettable here. It is not, on this product:
#   public/domain.te:535
#     neverallow { domain -init -vendor_init } vendor_default_prop:property_service set;
#   sits inside compatible_property_only(`...') (domain.te:528-536), and
#   public/te_macros:487 expands that macro to nothing unless
#   target_compatible_property is true or cts. This product sets
#   PRODUCT_SHIPPING_API_LEVEL := 26 (via product_launched_with_o.mk, inherited
#   at lineage_k50sv1_64_bsp.mk:78) and build/make/core/config.mk:645-652 only
#   raises PRODUCT_COMPATIBLE_PROPERTY to true when that level is > 27, so it
#   stays false. Confirmed against the built policy: the only occurrence of
#   `vendor_default_prop` next to `neverallow` in
#   out/.../sepolicy_neverallows_intermediates/policy.conf is inside a comment.
#   A plain allow rule on vendor_default_prop WOULD compile here.
# The types are kept anyway, for two reasons:
#   1. Least privilege. vendor_default_prop is the catch-all behind five plat
#      prefixes; granting set on it hands a domain every unlabelled vendor.*,
#      persist.vendor.*, ro.vendor.*, odm.* and ro.hardware.* property in the
#      image. A device type scopes the write to the prefixes one daemon owns,
#      which is the whole point of the split and is what survives if the
#      product ever raises its shipping API level.
#   2. The analogous assertion for the unnamespaced catch-all IS compiled and
#      is genuinely unfixable: public/domain.te:525
#        neverallow { domain -init -vendor_init } default_prop:property_service set;
#      sits OUTSIDE the compatible_property_only block and lands in the built
#      policy at policy.conf:11852. Any property this tree fails to label that
#      does not match a plat prefix falls there, and no allow rule can rescue
#      it -- so labelling is the only mechanism that works uniformly across
#      both catch-alls. Do not reason about one without the other.
type mtk_audiohal_prop, property_type;
